insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('finding-a-therapist', 'Finding a Therapist', 'You have decided to get help and now there are two hundred names and no way to choose. How to narrow it down, what to ask before you book, and how to tell early whether it is working.', 'You have got as far as deciding. Then you open a directory, and there are two hundred photographs, a dozen sets of letters after people''s names, and no way to tell any of them apart. So the tab stays open for three weeks and nothing happens.

The deciding was the hard part emotionally. This part is mostly logistics, and logistics respond to a method.

Start with the three things that rule people out

Most of the two hundred are not actually options, and finding that out first makes the choice much smaller.

Money. What can you pay per session, and how often. Then look specifically for sliding scale, training clinics attached to universities, community mental health services, employee assistance programs through work, and anything your insurance covers. Ask about the lowest fee they offer rather than assuming the listed one is fixed.

Practicality. In person or video. Which evenings or hours you can actually make, every week, for months. A brilliant therapist at a time you cannot keep is worse than a good one you can.

The thing you want help with. Panic, a bereavement, something that happened to you, a relationship, drinking, low mood that will not lift. Say it in your own words. You do not need the right term.

Then look for the match, not the best one

There is no best therapist, only a good fit for this problem and this person. Two things are worth matching on.

Experience with your particular thing. Someone who works with trauma, or with eating difficulties, or with couples, or with grief. This matters more than any general impression of warmth.

The approach, roughly. Cognitive behavioral therapy and its relatives are structured, practical, and strong for panic, phobias, low mood and worry. Trauma-focused approaches are what you want if something happened. More open-ended talking therapy suits long-running patterns and questions about who you are. If you have no idea, say the problem and ask them what they would suggest.

Check they are properly qualified

Titles are protected differently in different places, so look for a license or registration with a professional body rather than a certificate from a course. Where you live will have a register you can search.

That one check matters more than the website, the photograph or the writing.

Email or call three, not one

Pick three and contact all of them. Most will offer a short free call, and that call is the real selection process.

Three, because the first one may have no space, the second may not feel right, and lining them up at once stops this taking three months.

Ask these on the call

Have your questions written down, because people forget them when nervous.

Do you work with this a lot. What would the plan roughly look like. How long do people usually come for. What is the fee, and do you have any lower-cost spaces. What is your cancellation policy. Where is your waiting list at. How will we know whether it is working.

Then one that tells you most: what should I do if I do not think it is helping.

A good answer to that is warm and specific. Someone who seems uncomfortable with the question is answering it.

Expect to feel awkward on the call, and ignore that

The call is not an audition and you do not have to explain everything. Two or three sentences about the problem is plenty. If the thought of speaking at all is the obstacle, write an email instead, and say that you would rather start by email.

Judge it on the right things after a few sessions

The first session is mostly history and practicalities and often feels strange. By around the third or fourth, you can start to tell.

Good signs: you feel heard rather than assessed, there is some sense of a direction, you leave with something to think about or try, and you can imagine telling this person something embarrassing.

Reasons to raise it or change: you spend the session managing their reactions, nothing seems to be going anywhere after a couple of months, they talk about themselves a great deal, you feel judged, or you dread it in a way that is about them rather than about the work.

Not every discomfort is a bad sign. Therapy that goes anywhere is uncomfortable in places. The difference is whether the discomfort is the work or the person.

It is allowed to not work out

A fair number of people see two or three therapists before they land somewhere useful, and that is a normal part of this rather than a failure. You can say, plainly: I do not think this is the right fit, thank you for your time. You do not owe a reason, and a professional will not be wounded by it.

While you wait

Waiting lists are real and can be long. In the meantime: ask to go on more than one list, ask about cancellation slots, look at group programs which often start sooner, and ask your doctor what is available locally that does not need a referral.

When to get help

If you are struggling to get started on this at all, your doctor is the simplest first step: they can refer you, tell you what exists locally, and treat the things medication helps with while you wait. Say plainly how bad it is rather than the tidier version, because what is available often depends on that. If you are in crisis, do not wait for an appointment, and if you ever feel you cannot go on, please reach out to an urgent service today rather than a waiting list.', array['Decision Making', 'Worry']::text[], 0, '2026-10-26T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-a-sibling-stops-speaking-to-you', 'When a Sibling Stops Speaking to You', 'No row, no explanation, just silence that has lasted months. Living with an estranged brother or sister, what to try, what not to, and how to carry it when nothing changes.', 'There was no final argument you can point to. Messages got shorter, then stopped. They came to the family thing and spoke to everyone except you, or stopped coming at all. And when you ask what happened, you get nothing, or you get a sentence that does not match your memory of events.

It is a strange kind of loss, because they are not gone. They are on the internet. People mention them. And you are expected to carry on as though a person you grew up with has not disappeared out of your life.

This is more common than the silence suggests

Sibling estrangement is widespread and almost never talked about, which leaves most people in it feeling uniquely wrong. The quiet is not evidence of anything about you, and you are not the only person at the wedding managing this.

Grieve it, because it is a loss

What makes it harder than a death is that there is nothing to mark. No funeral, no condolences, nobody saying they are sorry. Just a hole you are supposed to be unbothered by.

Letting it be a grief changes things. You can be sad about the brother you had when you were ten, the family you thought you were in, and the uncle or aunt your children were going to have. That sadness is proportionate, and it does not require you to be in the wrong.

Try once, clearly, then stop trying constantly

Repeated messages usually entrench things, because the other person starts experiencing contact as pressure. One clear approach is worth more than thirty.

Keep it short, with no case made and no history relitigated. I would like to understand what happened, if you are ever willing to talk. I am not asking you to agree with me. I am here and the door is open.

Then stop, and let it be true. The open door is the message, and it keeps working without being repeated.

If they tell you why, listen before you defend

Sometimes a reason does arrive, and it is often something you remember very differently, or a version of your parents and your childhood you do not recognize.

The instinct is to correct it. That reliably ends the conversation. People with very different experiences of the same family are the norm rather than a sign that one of you is lying: birth order, who got what, who was ill, who was there for which years.

So you can say I did not know you experienced it that way, and I want to, without agreeing that everything they said is accurate. That sentence has reopened more of these than any argument ever has.

And if something you did is in there, apologize for that part cleanly, without the because.

Do not fight the war through other people

Asking your mother to intervene, or putting your side to cousins, nearly always makes it worse and puts people in an impossible position.

Expect to be managed around at family events. That is painful and it is not a verdict. Being civil in a kitchen for twenty minutes is an achievement rather than a failure to resolve anything.

Decide what you will say to everyone else

Have one short line ready, because you will be asked at every gathering for years. We are not in touch at the moment. It is sad and I would rather not go into it.

Said calmly, it stops the questions, and it does not hand anyone material.

Protect the parts that are still yours

If there are nieces and nephews you can stay close to, do, where it does not put them in the middle. If there are family traditions you loved, keep them. If their birthday guts you every year, plan that day rather than being ambushed by it.

And be careful about letting this become the whole story of your family. People in long estrangements often find their other relationships quietly narrow around it.

Accept that it may not resolve

Some of these end in reconciliation, often years later and usually prompted by an illness, a birth or a death. Some never do.

Living well while it is unresolved is a skill rather than a betrayal of hope. You can keep the door open and stop checking it every day.

If you are the one who stepped back

Sometimes the right thing is distance: where there was real harm, where contact is damaging, where someone is cruel or will not stop drinking or taking. Choosing not to be in contact with a sibling is allowed, and it is not the same as cutting someone off lightly. That decision usually still comes with grief, and it deserves the same care.

When to get help

Please talk to a therapist if this is sitting on you: if it dominates your thinking, if it has pulled your mood down for months, or if it has you questioning your memory of your own childhood. Estrangement is well understood by people who do family work, and they will not tell you to just call your brother. If this loss has left you hopeless, or you have had thoughts of not wanting to be here, please tell someone today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time. A sibling going quiet is a real loss, and you are allowed to treat it like one.', array['Loss', 'Loneliness']::text[], 0, '2026-10-26T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('being-the-one-who-holds-it-all-together', 'Being the One Who Holds It All Together', 'You are the reliable one, so everything lands on you, and nobody ever asks how you are. How the role gets built, what it costs, and how to put some of it down.', 'You are the one who gets called. You remember the appointments, you smooth it over at family dinners, you are the person at work people come to when something has gone wrong, and you are the one who notices that the trash needs taking out.

And nobody asks how you are, because you are the one who is fine. You have been fine so consistently that it has become a fact about you rather than a report.

How the role gets built

Nobody appoints you. It accumulates.

You are competent, so you get given more. You notice things other people do not, so you do them. You would rather do it yourself than watch it be done badly or late. Saying yes is quicker than the conversation about saying no. And somewhere in there, probably early, you learned that being useful is how you earn your place.

Then it becomes the system. Everyone around you organizes themselves around the fact that you will handle it, which is not malice. People take the path of least resistance, and you are it.

What it costs

The bill arrives quietly. Resentment that leaks out in small ways and then shames you. A tiredness that sleep does not fix. Feeling invisible in a crowd of people who rely on you. Being unable to enjoy anything because you are tracking everything.

And the loneliness, which is the part people least expect. Being the strong one means never being the one who is held, and that is a thin way to live even when it looks like strength from outside.

Notice who you are protecting from growth

This is the uncomfortable part. Holding everything up keeps other people from learning to carry their share.

The partner who does not know how the bills work. The sibling who has never once called the doctor. The colleague who brings you problems rather than solutions. None of them are going to develop that capacity while you are reliably doing it, and some of them have no idea it is a burden, because you have been so good at hiding the weight.

Start by stopping the pre-emptive rescue

Most of the load is not what you were asked to do. It is what you did before anyone asked, because you could see it coming.

So practice the pause. Let the silence sit after a problem is described. Do not fill it with I will sort it. See what the other person offers. A surprising amount of the time, somebody else moves, or it turns out not to need doing.

This is harder than saying no, and it is where most of the volume lives.

Hand things over in whole pieces

Help with the holidays does not work. One person owns the holiday meal does.

Give away a whole thing, with its decisions, and then genuinely leave it alone. It will be done differently and sometimes worse. If you step in to fix it, you have taken it back and taught everyone that handing it over is temporary.

Say the thing out loud, in plain words

People cannot respond to a hint, and the competent exterior is very convincing.

I am struggling and I need you to take some of this.

I cannot be the only person who notices what needs doing.

I need you to handle your own appointments from now on. I am not going to keep doing it.

Expect this to land badly at first, especially with people who have benefited from the arrangement. That is not a sign you were wrong to say it. Whole households and teams reorganize after sentences like these, and the first two weeks of a change are always the worst of it.

Be ready for the guilt, because it will come

Putting something down feels, from inside, like letting people down. You will get a wave of it the first few times.

Try a plainer description: I did my share and left theirs to them. And remember that the version of you that keeps going until something breaks is not more use to anyone. It is simply a later and bigger problem.

Get someone to hold you, too

Have one person whose job is not to be helped by you. A friend you tell the truth to, a therapist, a group. Being the strong one for everyone and having nowhere to put your own weight is the part that turns tiredness into something worse.

Start smaller than feels useful

One thing handed over. One pause instead of a rescue. One honest sentence. Whole-life rebalancing fails, and small changes stick, and three months of small changes is a different life.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor if you are exhausted in a way sleep does not touch, if you are tearful or irritable in a way that is not like you, or if you have stopped enjoying things, since that is burnout territory and it responds to treatment rather than to trying harder. A therapist is well worth it if you can see that being needed is how you decide you are worth something, because that belief is what rebuilds the role every time you put it down. You are allowed to be a person who sometimes needs looking after.', array['Burnout', 'Boundaries']::text[], 0, '2026-10-26T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('asking-for-more-money-at-work', 'Asking for More Money at Work', 'You know you are underpaid and the thought of raising it makes you feel sick. How to prepare, what to actually say, and how to handle a no without it becoming a verdict on you.', 'You have done the work. You have probably been doing more of it than your title says for a while. And you have rehearsed the conversation in the shower eleven times and never had it, because every version of it in your head ends in humiliation.

So the year goes by, and the small raise arrives automatically, and the feeling of being quietly taken for granted settles in deeper.

Why this is harder than other requests

Money at work sits on top of something tender: the belief that you are worth something, and a fear that asking will reveal that you are not.

People also confuse two separate things. What you are worth as a person, which is not a number, and what the market pays for this work in this place, which is a number and has nothing to do with your character. Keeping those apart makes the conversation enormously easier.

Find out the actual number first

Walking in without data turns the conversation into a feeling, and feelings lose.

Look at job listings for your role in your area, right now. Ask people in your field, including a recruiter, who will tell you plainly what they place people at. Look at the salary band for your level if your organization publishes them. Check what the job would be advertised at if you left tomorrow.

Then pick a specific figure rather than a vague more, and have a slightly higher ask with a number you would accept behind it.

Build the case on what you did, not on what you need

Your rent going up is real and it is not an argument your manager can take to anyone. What they need is a case they can repeat upward.

So write down four or five things, with specifics. The thing you now run that you did not run a year ago. The work you absorbed when someone left. A number if you have one: money brought in, time saved, customers kept, errors prevented. The things people come to you for.

Then one sentence tying it together: I am doing a bigger job than I was hired for, and I am paid below the market for it.

Ask for the meeting properly

Do not raise it in a corridor or at the end of an unrelated call. Book time and say what it is for.

I would like twenty minutes to talk about my pay. Giving notice is not a threat, it is a courtesy, and it also means they arrive prepared instead of defensive.

Timing helps. Budgets get set at particular points, and your manager usually knows when. Asking a month before the cycle beats asking a week after it closes.

Say the ask plainly and then be quiet

Open with it rather than building up for five minutes.

Based on what I am doing now and what the market pays, I am asking for X. Here is the case.

Then stop. Let the silence be theirs. People talk themselves down in the pause, adding or maybe a bit less, or it is fine if not. Do not negotiate against yourself before they have said a word. Nothing you add in that silence will help you.

A no is usually about the system

Most noes are about budget cycles, bands, a freeze, or someone above your manager, and almost none are a judgment about your worth.

So treat a no as the start of the next question: what would need to be true for this to be a yes, and when can we look at it again. Ask for specifics, write them down, and send a short note afterward confirming what you both said. That note is what makes the second conversation much easier.

If money is genuinely impossible, other things have value: a title change, a training budget, a day at home, time off, a defined path to the next level. A title change in particular is often cheap to give and worth real money the next time you move.

Do not let it curdle quietly

If the answer is no with nothing behind it, and this has happened repeatedly, that is information. The strongest position in any pay conversation is knowing what you would do if the answer is no, which usually means knowing what else is out there.

You do not have to threaten anything. You only have to stop feeling trapped, and quietly looking is what does that.

Separate the outcome from how you did

People leave these conversations deciding they were humiliating when in fact they went fine. Rate the asking, not the answer. You booked it, you said a number, you stayed in the room. That is the part you controlled and it gets markedly easier the second time.

When to get help

If dread of this conversation is keeping you in a job that underpays you, or if the thought of asking for anything brings on a level of fear that is out of proportion, that is worth taking to a therapist, since it is usually part of a wider pattern with asking and it responds well to practice with someone. Please talk to your doctor if money worry is keeping you awake or has pulled your mood down for weeks, and if the underpayment is tied to your sex, race, age or disability, get proper advice from a union or an employment adviser rather than treating it as a confidence problem.', array['Assertiveness', 'Self-Esteem']::text[], 0, '2026-10-26T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('taking-a-break-from-drinking', 'Taking a Break From Drinking', 'You are not asking whether to quit forever, only what a few weeks without it would be like. How to give a break a fair chance, and what the first two weeks usually tell you.', 'You are not in crisis. You are drinking most evenings, more than you planned on some of them, and you have started wondering what you would feel like without it for a while.

That is a good question and it deserves a real test rather than a vague intention to cut down, which is the version that quietly becomes nothing.

Pick a length and write it down

Cutting down is not a plan, because every evening becomes a negotiation. A defined break takes the deciding out of each night, which is where the effort actually goes.

Two weeks tells you something. Four tells you more, because the sleep and the mood changes mostly show up in the second half. Pick a start date in the next few days rather than next month, and tell one person.

Before you start, check one thing with a doctor

This part matters. If you drink heavily and daily, stopping suddenly can be dangerous, with shaking, sweating, sickness, hallucinations or seizures.

If you drink every day and more than a little, if you have ever had withdrawal symptoms, if you have a drink in the morning or to steady yourself, or if you are physically unwell, talk to a doctor before you stop rather than during. A managed reduction is safer and they will not lecture you.

Work out what the drink is doing for you

A break works much better when you know what you are replacing, because the function is the thing that defeats willpower.

For most people it is one of a few jobs. Marking the end of the day. Taking the edge off anxiety. Getting to sleep. Being able to talk at social things. Rewarding yourself. Numbing something specific.

Write down which it is for you, honestly, and then plan a substitute for that particular job rather than a general resolve to be better. An end-of-day ritual, a walk, a shower, a non-alcoholic drink you actually like in a proper glass, a call with someone.

Expect the first week to be unimpressive

People start a break expecting to feel fantastic and give up on day five when they feel worse. Early on you may sleep badly, feel irritable, and want it more in the evenings than you expected.

The good parts tend to arrive in week two and three: sleeping through, waking without the low-grade dread, a clearer head in the mornings, and often a surprising amount of extra time.

Plan the hard evenings in advance

Decide now what happens at the three predictable pressure points.

The first evening at home when the urge arrives at six o''clock. Have the substitute ready and in the house.

The first social event. Decide what you will drink, get it in your hand early, and have one short line ready: I am off it for a few weeks. No reason needed and no debate entered.

The first bad day, which is where most breaks end. Know in advance what you will do instead, and that one drink does not have to end the experiment.

Keep a line a day

A note each evening: did I drink, how I slept, mood out of ten, anything notable. It takes ten seconds and it is the whole point of the exercise, because at the end you will have evidence rather than an impression.

What the break is actually telling you

At the end, read your notes and ask three plain questions.

Was it easier or harder than you expected. Harder than expected is useful information rather than a failure.

What got better. Sleep, mood, mornings, money, weight of dread.

What happened when you could not have one. Relief at the end, irritation, planning around it, or genuinely not minding.

If you could not get through more than a day or two, if you felt physically unwell, or if the break was so difficult that you stopped early more than once, that is worth a conversation with a doctor rather than another try on your own.

Decide what happens next, deliberately

Most people land in one of three places. Back to as before, which at least you now know is a choice rather than a default. A lower, more deliberate amount, with some days off each week. Or realizing they would rather not, which a surprising number of people find once the first few weeks are behind them.

Any of those is a result. The thing you have gained either way is a decision you are making on purpose.

Do not let one evening end it

If you drink on day nine, you have not failed the whole thing. Write it down, notice what was happening, and carry on tomorrow. The all-or-nothing version of this is what makes people give up for years.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor before stopping if you drink daily or heavily, since withdrawal can be medically serious and is manageable with support. Talk to them too if you have tried to cut back several times without it holding, if you drink to steady yourself, if it is affecting your work or your relationships, or if you are drinking to get away from something heavy like grief or a frightening memory. There is a great deal of effective help, including counseling and medication, and asking about a break is a completely reasonable way into that conversation.', array['Motivation', 'Goals']::text[], 0, '2026-10-26T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
on conflict (slug) do update set
  title        = excluded.title,
  excerpt      = excluded.excerpt,
  body_md      = excluded.body_md,
  tags         = excluded.tags,
  min_level    = 0,
  published_at = excluded.published_at;
